1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,160
The next section that we're going to cover

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:04,160
is free radical injury.

3
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:08,080
And the way this fits into the chapter

4
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:10,960
is that we've been talking about cellular injury

5
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:15,080
and we discussed hypoxia as one of the important causes

6
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:16,520
of cellular injury.

7
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:19,240
And of course, we used hypoxia as a mechanism

8
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:22,320
to teach some of the principles that a cell undergoes

9
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:23,800
when it becomes injured.

10
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:26,920
So we're now going to continue with our discussion of injury.

11
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:29,720
However, instead, we're gonna focus on free radicals,

12
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:32,400
which can also cause injury.

13
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:35,620
These are relatively, this discussion is relatively high yield

14
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:40,220
and hence the reason why I've made it a separate

15
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:42,160
sort of subsection in your text.

16
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:45,960
All right, before we can begin our discussion of free radicals,

17
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:47,480
we should take a step back

18
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:50,320
and define what a free radical actually is.

19
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:53,020
So a free radical is a chemical species

20
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:57,740
with an unpaired electron in the outer orbit.

21
00:00:57,740 --> 00:01:05,640
And it's this unpaired electron that has the ability to induce damage within the cell.

22
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:11,260
Now, it's important to recognize that free radicals are actually generated physiologically

23
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:12,420
and pathologically.

24
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:16,100
So what do we mean by physiologically?

25
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:23,700
Well, physiologic generation of free radicals occurs during oxidative phosphorylation.

26
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:27,100
And of course, this is part of the normal physiology of a human being.

27
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:29,100
So that's why we use the term physiologic.

28
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:32,600
Now, let's review oxidative phosphorylation briefly

29
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:37,080
and remind ourselves that during oxidative phosphorylation,

30
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:41,860
cytochrome C oxidase transfers electrons to oxygen as a final electron acceptor.

31
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:46,240
And you recall in our prior discussion of the electron transport chain,

32
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:48,920
oxygen obviously accepts electrons,

33
00:01:49,140 --> 00:01:54,240
which then drives the production of a proton gradient,

34
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:57,100
which then eventually drives the production of ATP.

35
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:04,420
Now, generally speaking, oxygen will actually accept four electrons.

36
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:08,500
And after accepting four electrons, water will be generated.

37
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:14,760
And so if during the sequence of accepting of electrons,

38
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:18,620
oxygen is partially reduced and doesn't receive all four electrons,

39
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:21,460
then free radicals can be generated.

40
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:25,120
But in order to understand the free radicals that are generated,

41
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:27,300
we do have to review a little bit of biochemistry.

42
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:31,340
So let's take a moment to just remind ourselves that if this is oxygen here,

43
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:35,100
if oxygen accepts one electron,

44
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:38,080
then it becomes something called superoxide.

45
00:02:39,540 --> 00:02:41,540
If it accepts two electrons,

46
00:02:42,260 --> 00:02:44,520
it then becomes hydrogen peroxide.

47
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:48,540
If it accepts three electrons,

48
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:53,340
it becomes hydroxyl ion.

49
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:57,160
And if it becomes,

50
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:59,000
if it accepts four electrons,

51
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:01,140
it becomes water.

52
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:07,280
And so when I say that physiologic generation of free radicals

53
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:08,980
occurs during oxidative phosphorylation,

54
00:03:09,120 --> 00:03:12,040
obviously if there is a partial reduction of oxygen

55
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:14,580
so that it only accepts one, two, or three electrons

56
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:17,060
instead of the normal four electrons,

57
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:19,620
that's going to result in the production of free radicals.

58
00:03:19,780 --> 00:03:21,740
And that does happen physiologically.

59
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:26,880
Free radicals can also be generated pathologically.

60
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:29,020
Some important examples,

61
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:32,640
ionizing radiation can generate free radicals.

62
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:36,200
And ionizing radiation,

63
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:38,420
the classic free radical that's generated,

64
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:39,880
is actually hydroxyl ion,

65
00:03:40,380 --> 00:03:41,040
so OH.

66
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:44,620
And what's important to remember,

67
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:47,520
to recall here is that ionizing radiation,

68
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:49,640
the way by which it generates these free radicals

69
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:54,500
is that the radiation actually hits water within tissues.

70
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:57,560
And once it hits water within tissues,

71
00:03:57,720 --> 00:03:59,340
one possible consequence of that

72
00:03:59,340 --> 00:04:02,340
is the generation of hydroxyl free radical.

73
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:05,100
And we should bear in mind that

74
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:08,200
among all of the free radicals,

75
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:14,680
the hydroxyl free radical is sort of the most damaging.

76
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:17,200
And that's something that examiners like you to know.

77
00:04:17,620 --> 00:04:18,440
So again, I'll repeat it.

78
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:19,960
Of all of the free radicals,

79
00:04:20,040 --> 00:04:23,780
the hydroxyl free radical is the most damaging.

80
00:04:24,620 --> 00:04:29,220
Now, another mechanism by which free radicals can be generated

81
00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:31,460
or are generated actually is inflammation.

82
00:04:32,340 --> 00:04:37,480
Remind ourselves that when neutrophils come in to battle an infection,

83
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:42,340
they have two mechanisms of killing the microbe.

84
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:45,260
One is called oxygen-dependent.

85
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:47,460
The other is oxygen-independent.

86
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:52,200
Remember that the oxygen-dependent mechanism starts out,

87
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:54,260
if you go back to biochemistry,

88
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:57,640
the reaction starts out with something called an oxidative burst.

89
00:04:57,640 --> 00:05:01,120
And basically what happens there is that oxygen is taken,

90
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:05,480
and oxygen is acted on by an enzyme.

91
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:07,400
And do you remember the name of that enzyme?

92
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:10,440
It's important for biochemistry, high yield for examinations.

93
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:16,100
The enzyme is called NADPH oxidase.

94
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:21,120
And NADPH oxidase takes oxygen and converts it to superoxide.

95
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:24,320
And so that's the very first step.

96
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:30,620
And superoxide will very quickly be converted by superoxide dismutase to hydrogen peroxide,

97
00:05:30,680 --> 00:05:38,020
and then hydrogen peroxide is converted by myeloperoxidase to form bleach, or HOCl.

98
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:46,220
But we'll discuss the additional reactions later when we do our chapter or section on inflammation.

99
00:05:46,220 --> 00:05:50,660
However, what I really want to highlight here is this very first reaction.

100
00:05:51,220 --> 00:05:57,000
That first reaction in an oxygen-dependent killing generates free radical,

101
00:05:57,360 --> 00:06:00,040
and so in particular the superoxide free radical.

102
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:05,700
And so inflammation is another mechanism by which free radicals are formed.

103
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:10,860
A third pathologic mechanism by which free radicals can form

104
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:15,560
is by interaction with metals, for example, copper and iron.

105
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,580
And this actually becomes very high yield as well.

106
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:22,860
The reason we have to be familiar with this is because,

107
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:24,640
well, why don't we take a step back.

108
00:06:24,700 --> 00:06:27,180
First of all, let's remind ourselves from biochemistry

109
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:30,940
that generally speaking, copper and iron are tightly,

110
00:06:32,040 --> 00:06:34,900
are usually whenever present in the body, are usually bound.

111
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:36,280
Just to give you an example,

112
00:06:36,280 --> 00:06:40,940
I mean, you're all familiar with the headaches of learning about iron biochemistry

113
00:06:40,940 --> 00:06:45,420
and the concept of, you know, ferritin and transferrin

114
00:06:45,420 --> 00:06:49,260
and all these molecules that iron gets bound to the moment that it enters the blood.

115
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:54,540
The reason iron is so carefully bound the moment it enters the blood

116
00:06:54,540 --> 00:06:56,460
is because if iron is left free,

117
00:06:57,040 --> 00:07:00,540
it has the ability to generate free radicals.

118
00:07:00,540 --> 00:07:04,040
And the way by which it generates the free radical,

119
00:07:04,240 --> 00:07:06,980
there's an important reaction, the name of which you should be aware of.

120
00:07:07,060 --> 00:07:08,560
It's called the Fenton reaction.

121
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:13,100
And the Fenton reaction allows for iron to actually generate a free radical.

122
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:18,000
And the free radical that is most commonly generated is hydroxyl free radical.

123
00:07:18,120 --> 00:07:23,580
And again, hydroxyl free radical is the most dangerous of all of the free radicals.

124
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:27,400
Maybe it'd be easier for you to remember if we just,

125
00:07:27,400 --> 00:07:31,660
or at least for an examination, perhaps another important point here,

126
00:07:31,960 --> 00:07:33,600
which will help you remember this concept,

127
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:37,740
is we have a couple diseases in which either copper can build up

128
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:39,680
or in which iron can build up.

129
00:07:40,020 --> 00:07:43,500
And in both of those diseases, when copper and iron build up,

130
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:46,100
we get various clinical manifestations.

131
00:07:46,720 --> 00:07:48,160
Let's just take iron, for example.

132
00:07:48,160 --> 00:07:51,140
When iron builds up, we get a disease called hemochromatosis.

133
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:54,340
And you're well aware of the fact that hemochromatosis,

134
00:07:54,340 --> 00:07:59,560
when hemochromatosis results in tissue damage, for example,

135
00:07:59,940 --> 00:08:03,780
can create cirrhosis in the liver and damage in other tissues.

136
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:07,500
And that's the reason or the underlying mechanism behind that tissue damage

137
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:10,900
is actually the fact that that excess iron in the body

138
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:14,620
that's seen in hemochromatosis actually generates free radicals.

139
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:18,880
Another example, I just take an excess of copper.

140
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:21,660
So what's the disease in which there is an excess of copper?

141
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:23,800
It's called Wilson's disease.

142
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:27,940
And in Wilson's disease, the excess of copper generates free radicals,

143
00:08:27,940 --> 00:08:29,440
which then leads to tissue damage,

144
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:34,620
creating a certain clinical, a series or set of certain clinical symptoms.

145
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:36,740
So again, it's important to know,

146
00:08:36,920 --> 00:08:40,280
and examiners love to go after the, to ask you,

147
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:42,880
what's the underlying mechanism of damage in hemochromatosis,

148
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:47,960
or what's the underlying mechanism of damage in, um, in Wilson's disease.

149
00:08:48,140 --> 00:08:51,640
In both cases, it's the pathologic generation of free radicals

150
00:08:51,640 --> 00:08:53,940
that actually results in the tissue damage

151
00:08:53,940 --> 00:08:56,060
that then produces clinical symptoms.

152
00:08:57,420 --> 00:09:00,620
All right, another example of pathologic generation of free radicals

153
00:09:00,620 --> 00:09:01,540
are drugs and chemicals.

154
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:05,840
Acetaminophen is a, is a very kind of classic example,

155
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:07,280
especially from pharmacology.

156
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:09,700
Acetaminophen, um, goes to the liver.

157
00:09:09,860 --> 00:09:11,900
It gets converted by the P450 system.

158
00:09:11,900 --> 00:09:15,400
And, uh, free radicals are, are generated in the process.

159
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:19,400
Um, the, the free radicals can be damaging to the tissues.

160
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:22,020
And you, you're, you will have learned,

161
00:09:22,120 --> 00:09:23,880
or perhaps have already learned,

162
00:09:24,220 --> 00:09:26,900
that high dose, um, acetaminophen can be,

163
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:29,740
or acetaminophen can be taken by a patient, uh,

164
00:09:30,320 --> 00:09:32,400
who might try to commit suicide, for example,

165
00:09:32,620 --> 00:09:34,640
or who may overdose on acetaminophen.

166
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:38,880
And that overdose of acetaminophen causes massive liver necrosis.

167
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:41,440
And the massive liver necrosis is due to the fact

168
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:43,320
that the acetaminophen gets into the liver,

169
00:09:43,460 --> 00:09:44,740
the hepatocytes convert it,

170
00:09:44,900 --> 00:09:47,160
and in the process, free radicals are generated.

171
00:09:47,620 --> 00:09:50,240
And so that results in, in damage in the liver.

172
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,940
Um, a, a chemical that can cause, uh, free radical,

173
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:57,560
um, or a classic chemical that can become a free radical

174
00:09:57,560 --> 00:10:00,300
is carbon tetrachloride.

175
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:04,080
And carbon tetrachloride, I'm going to discuss in much more detail

176
00:10:04,080 --> 00:10:07,160
in a moment, because it lends to a high-yield principle

177
00:10:07,160 --> 00:10:08,280
that shows up on exam.

178
00:10:08,440 --> 00:10:10,320
So we'll, we'll, let's put that one aside for a moment,

179
00:10:10,380 --> 00:10:13,120
and we'll discuss it, again, in just a few slides.

180
00:10:14,220 --> 00:10:16,260
Um, what's the problem with free radicals?

181
00:10:16,260 --> 00:10:16,960
I mean, so what?

182
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:18,480
If you get a free radical, how?

183
00:10:18,660 --> 00:10:21,340
It, I've told you it injures the cell, but how?

184
00:10:21,940 --> 00:10:25,340
So, uh, free radicals can do a couple things.

185
00:10:25,460 --> 00:10:27,480
Number one, they peroxidize lipids,

186
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:31,400
and that is damaging, particularly, to the lipid membrane.

187
00:10:31,400 --> 00:10:34,180
Uh, and number two, they have the ability to oxidize,

188
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:37,240
and in particular, they can oxidize, oxidize DNA and proteins.

189
00:10:37,700 --> 00:10:42,540
The oxid, oxidation of proteins will result in, um, in, in,

190
00:10:42,540 --> 00:10:44,180
in damage to the cell.

191
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:47,380
And also, the oxidation of DNA can result in damage to the cell.

192
00:10:47,460 --> 00:10:50,640
And this is important because it's particularly implicated

193
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:51,640
in oncogenesis.

194
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:52,680
What's oncogenesis?

195
00:10:52,860 --> 00:10:54,700
The, the formation of cancers.

196
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:58,660
Um, remember that cancers are formed by mutations in DNA.

197
00:10:58,660 --> 00:11:02,000
Okay, and if there's a, uh, if there's oxidation of DNA,

198
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:03,980
that would introduce mutations within DNA,

199
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:06,980
and so it would increase, it would, it could potentially cause cancer.

200
00:11:07,520 --> 00:11:11,040
And, and this is the whole idea behind, uh, uh, for example,

201
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:14,600
if you walk into a vitamin store, or you look at an advertisement for vitamins,

202
00:11:14,600 --> 00:11:18,780
where they say that, uh, you know, antioxidants and other things

203
00:11:18,780 --> 00:11:22,920
that fight off free radicals can prevent aging and protect you from cancer,

204
00:11:22,920 --> 00:11:26,900
and that's because there's, uh, there's an understanding that, um,

205
00:11:26,900 --> 00:11:32,120
that free radicals have the ability to damage DNA, which, uh, which can, um,

206
00:11:32,120 --> 00:11:36,800
lead to an increased risk for cancer, and they also have the ability to damage the cell,

207
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:42,600
which can potentially add to, uh, or lead to, let's say, be a component of aging.

208
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:48,640
Now, because free radicals are so damaging, and because they're generated physiologically,

209
00:11:48,900 --> 00:11:53,680
it only makes sense that the body would have a mechanism of eliminating the free radicals

210
00:11:53,680 --> 00:11:57,280
so that damage would not occur, especially since they're generated physiologically.

211
00:11:57,500 --> 00:12:00,320
So, of, of course, if we're going to generate them physiologically,

212
00:12:00,440 --> 00:12:02,420
then we need to be able to eliminate them as well.

213
00:12:03,060 --> 00:12:06,660
There are actually three major mechanisms by which, uh, free radicals can be eliminated.

214
00:12:06,920 --> 00:12:12,200
The first are antioxidants, and antioxidants, um, include things like, um,

215
00:12:12,460 --> 00:12:17,140
vitamin A, vitamin C, vitamin E, um, these are your classic antioxidants,

216
00:12:17,380 --> 00:12:21,300
and, and they have the ability to, to, um, to eliminate free radicals.

217
00:12:21,300 --> 00:12:25,940
Um, another, uh, important mechanism of elimination of free radicals,

218
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:29,820
I'm going to skip here and go a little out of order, but it's metal carrier proteins.

219
00:12:30,440 --> 00:12:33,420
Um, metal carrier proteins are what I've already talked about.

220
00:12:33,520 --> 00:12:37,880
You, you know that copper and iron have the ability to create free radicals,

221
00:12:37,940 --> 00:12:41,080
iron in particular, by the Fenton reaction, um,

222
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:44,880
and the generation of free radicals, um, is, is a dangerous thing.

223
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:48,900
And so, the way by which the body handles that is to have metal carrier proteins.

224
00:12:49,040 --> 00:12:55,500
For example, transferrin in the blood, which tightly binds iron to deliver it to the liver and the macrophages.

225
00:12:55,840 --> 00:13:01,280
And you know that when iron is actually bound in, um, macrophages or within the liver,

226
00:13:01,440 --> 00:13:05,140
it's actually tightly bound to a molecule called ferritin.

227
00:13:05,700 --> 00:13:11,700
And so, these metal carrier proteins, they protect, uh, they basically hide away the metals

228
00:13:11,700 --> 00:13:13,520
so that free radicals are not generated.

229
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:20,580
The third category of, um, of, um, substances that can eliminate free radicals are enzymes.

230
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:26,100
Now, the reason why I sort of take on this category last is because there's a little detail here

231
00:13:26,100 --> 00:13:29,440
from biochemistry that examiners expect you to be familiar with.

232
00:13:29,940 --> 00:13:32,660
So, let's, let's briefly talk about, um, the enzymes.

233
00:13:33,180 --> 00:13:34,720
There are three major enzymes.

234
00:13:34,980 --> 00:13:39,640
Each of them is relatively high yield that are important for the elimination of free radicals.

235
00:13:39,640 --> 00:13:46,100
Um, I, I think that the easy way for me, at least, to, to, to remember these is to go back

236
00:13:46,100 --> 00:13:50,040
and just create that same biochemical, um, paradigm that I highlighted earlier,

237
00:13:50,200 --> 00:13:55,600
where oxygen, if it accepts one electron, um, it becomes superoxide.

238
00:13:56,080 --> 00:13:59,140
So, superoxide is an important free radical to be familiar with.

239
00:13:59,140 --> 00:14:04,740
Uh, super, if it were to accept another electron from here, it would become hydrogen peroxide,

240
00:14:04,740 --> 00:14:09,260
H2O2, which is another important free radical to be familiar with.

241
00:14:09,620 --> 00:14:15,640
Hydrogen peroxide, were it to, were it to accept another electron, for example, would, um,

242
00:14:15,900 --> 00:14:17,840
would actually become hydroxyl free radical.

243
00:14:18,780 --> 00:14:20,660
Sorry, hydroxyl free radical.

244
00:14:21,100 --> 00:14:23,460
And obviously this could eventually generate water.

245
00:14:24,020 --> 00:14:29,920
Um, now, I use this paradigm to help me to remember, um, the, each of the free radicals,

246
00:14:29,920 --> 00:14:33,900
because these are the three key free radicals that you must be familiar with from biochemistry.

247
00:14:34,420 --> 00:14:37,380
And then I use this paradigm to remind me of, um, the enzymes,

248
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:41,840
because each of these free radicals here, the one, you know, the three that I've drawn here,

249
00:14:42,100 --> 00:14:46,240
are actually each handled by one, uh, particular enzyme, or predominantly by one enzyme.

250
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:47,760
So, the first one is easy.

251
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:51,160
This is, this one is superoxide, and guess who handles this?

252
00:14:51,520 --> 00:14:52,800
Superoxide dismutase.

253
00:14:53,160 --> 00:14:56,300
So, superoxide dismutase actually takes out superoxide.

254
00:14:56,300 --> 00:15:02,400
Um, hydrogen peroxide is, um, is handled by catalase.

255
00:15:03,800 --> 00:15:04,360
All right.

256
00:15:04,840 --> 00:15:06,580
Oops, it's handled by catalase.

257
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:12,620
And then hydroxyl free radical, um, they, the, one of the major mechanisms by which this,

258
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:17,580
uh, free radicals actually removed is glutathione peroxidase.

259
00:15:18,620 --> 00:15:24,140
Um, and glutathione peroxidase is an enzyme that takes the assistance of glutathione,

260
00:15:24,140 --> 00:15:31,320
which is an antioxidant, um, and, uh, glutathione, um, will be very important, uh,

261
00:15:31,320 --> 00:15:35,120
later in our discussions of, um, uh, in our discussions of anemia.

262
00:15:35,340 --> 00:15:37,580
And you'll, you'll hear about that when we talk about anemia,

263
00:15:37,720 --> 00:15:40,340
so I won't, uh, get into too much detail here.

264
00:15:40,760 --> 00:15:45,560
However, it is important to know these three, the, the, the three key free radicals,

265
00:15:45,560 --> 00:15:49,600
again, superoxide, hydrogen peroxide, and hydroxyl free radical.

266
00:15:50,140 --> 00:15:52,820
It's important to know that hydroxyl free radical is the most damaging,

267
00:15:53,020 --> 00:15:56,180
or the most sort of dangerous of the three free radicals.

268
00:15:56,480 --> 00:16:01,920
And then it's important to know what enzymes, um, disrupt or, or handle each of those free radicals.

269
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:05,760
And clearly superoxide would be handled by superoxide dismutase,

270
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
and hydrogen peroxide by catalase,

271
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:11,860
and then hydroxyl free radical by glutathione peroxidase.

272
00:16:11,860 --> 00:16:16,720
All right. Now, to, to close off this discussion,

273
00:16:16,920 --> 00:16:21,280
we want to talk about two very high-yield, um, examples of free radical injury

274
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:22,800
that often show up on an examination.

275
00:16:23,360 --> 00:16:26,260
So, the first is carbon tetrachloride.

276
00:16:26,340 --> 00:16:30,720
And carbon tetrachloride is, was classically seen in the dry cleaning industry.

277
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:34,760
So, uh, a clinical vignette might say that the person is exposed to the dry,

278
00:16:34,880 --> 00:16:36,800
the chemicals of dry cleaning industry.

279
00:16:37,240 --> 00:16:41,800
Now, carbon tetrachloride, um, happens to get into the blood,

280
00:16:41,860 --> 00:16:46,640
and when it gets into the blood, it gets converted to something called carbon trichloride.

281
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:50,880
It's converted by carbon trichloride, which is a free radical.

282
00:16:51,360 --> 00:16:57,800
It's converted by, it's converted to carbon trichloride in the P450 system of the liver.

283
00:16:58,000 --> 00:16:58,680
That's important.

284
00:16:58,920 --> 00:17:01,100
In the P450 system of the liver.

285
00:17:01,100 --> 00:17:04,740
Once it's converted to carbon trichloride, that's now a free radical,

286
00:17:04,940 --> 00:17:07,560
and that damages the, uh, the hepatocytes.

287
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:11,560
The initial stage of damage will actually be reversible,

288
00:17:11,560 --> 00:17:15,280
and the key sign of reversible damage will be cellular swelling.

289
00:17:15,660 --> 00:17:22,040
When the cell swells up, um, one of the consequences will be that the endoplasmic,

290
00:17:22,040 --> 00:17:25,480
the rough endoplasmic reticulum, uh, will also swell,

291
00:17:25,900 --> 00:17:28,640
and when that swells, ribosomes will pop off,

292
00:17:28,900 --> 00:17:31,520
and protein synthesis will be reduced.

293
00:17:31,940 --> 00:17:33,900
Now, if protein synthesis is reduced,

294
00:17:34,060 --> 00:17:36,880
then you need to take a step back and just think about your biochemistry

295
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:39,620
and physiology and histology, etc.,

296
00:17:39,620 --> 00:17:44,480
and remind yourself that, um, one of the very important functions of the liver

297
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:47,320
is to gather fat, particularly from the diet,

298
00:17:47,720 --> 00:17:51,000
and then to bring, and then to repackage it and send it back out.

299
00:17:51,320 --> 00:17:57,800
Now, the repackaging of fat actually occurs by binding up the molecules of cholesterol and lipids

300
00:17:57,800 --> 00:18:01,400
to, um, uh, via apolipoproteins.

301
00:18:01,900 --> 00:18:06,560
Um, remember that if you're going to produce, um, the, the, um,

302
00:18:06,680 --> 00:18:10,280
you're going to be able to produce the, uh, the, not the molecule,

303
00:18:10,420 --> 00:18:13,740
but the substance that's going to carry, um, fat and cholesterol,

304
00:18:13,740 --> 00:18:15,480
you're going to need apolipoproteins.

305
00:18:15,880 --> 00:18:20,160
So, if carbon tetrachloride damages the liver cell and causes swelling,

306
00:18:20,280 --> 00:18:23,040
and swelling causes, uh, loss of rough,

307
00:18:23,180 --> 00:18:25,900
or loss of the ribosomes on the endoplasmic reticulum,

308
00:18:25,980 --> 00:18:28,020
which then causes a decrease in protein synthesis,

309
00:18:28,660 --> 00:18:33,100
the decrease in protein synthesis will then eventually result in lack of apolipoproteins.

310
00:18:33,460 --> 00:18:37,180
Lack of apolipoproteins means that fat comes into the liver but can't get out.

311
00:18:37,620 --> 00:18:40,840
So, guess what the classic histologic finding will be?

312
00:18:41,460 --> 00:18:42,800
Fatty change in the liver.

313
00:18:42,800 --> 00:18:45,360
And that's very, very high yield.

314
00:18:45,860 --> 00:18:49,160
Uh, because this goes back to a total tracing of, um,

315
00:18:49,540 --> 00:18:54,380
or a total retracing of the biochemistry behind cellular, cellular injury.

316
00:18:54,700 --> 00:18:55,860
So, let me show you a picture.

317
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:57,260
This is a classic image.

318
00:18:57,600 --> 00:18:59,240
Uh, this, these are hepatocytes.

319
00:18:59,400 --> 00:19:02,020
Each of these individual cells are, are hepatocytes.

320
00:19:02,220 --> 00:19:06,840
And you can clearly see that there are all these circular spaces within these hepatocytes.

321
00:19:06,900 --> 00:19:09,680
And these circular spaces represent the accumulation of fat,

322
00:19:09,680 --> 00:19:10,840
which is called fatty liver.

323
00:19:11,520 --> 00:19:17,700
Now, uh, again, the fatty liver is, uh, occurs, or the, we can say the mechanism of the fatty liver here,

324
00:19:17,700 --> 00:19:19,880
is decreased protein synthesis.

325
00:19:20,140 --> 00:19:24,480
In particular, decreased proteins, decreased synthesis of apolipoproteins,

326
00:19:24,620 --> 00:19:29,080
so that fat begins to accumulate in the cells and really can't be transported out of the cells.

327
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:31,960
Um, and so that's something to be very familiar with.

328
00:19:32,060 --> 00:19:33,380
Again, relatively high yield.

329
00:19:34,080 --> 00:19:36,760
I'm going to take you a step back, or one slide back.

330
00:19:37,080 --> 00:19:43,160
And now, finally, end off this discussion with, uh, with, with another example of free radical injury,

331
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:44,920
which is called reperfusion injury.

332
00:19:44,920 --> 00:19:51,140
The idea behind reperfusion injury is that, um, when you cut the blood supply to,

333
00:19:51,140 --> 00:19:56,520
let's say that the blood supply is cut off to an organ, um, I don't know, just take the heart, for example,

334
00:19:56,640 --> 00:20:01,660
the cardiac, sorry, the coronary artery gets secluded, there's a myocardial infarction,

335
00:20:01,780 --> 00:20:03,180
and the tissue begins to die.

336
00:20:04,120 --> 00:20:10,500
Now, once the tissue dies, um, the cell membrane is going to be damaged, right?

337
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:14,680
That's the hallmark of irreversible injury, cell membrane damage,

338
00:20:15,040 --> 00:20:17,260
and enzymes will begin to leak out into the blood.

339
00:20:17,380 --> 00:20:22,220
In particular, the, the troponins are sort of the gold standard enzyme, um,

340
00:20:22,300 --> 00:20:24,820
cardiac troponins that will actually leak out into the blood.

341
00:20:25,220 --> 00:20:28,240
Now, uh, as those enzymes leak out into the blood,

342
00:20:28,340 --> 00:20:32,920
there will be an indication that there has been irreversible injury to the cells.

343
00:20:33,880 --> 00:20:39,700
If, um, the blood is returned to the, um, organ,

344
00:20:39,700 --> 00:20:44,020
let's say that the patient goes to the cath lab and they open up the, the artery,

345
00:20:44,480 --> 00:20:46,900
and when they open up the artery, blood goes back to the organ,

346
00:20:47,260 --> 00:20:52,100
one of the, um, interesting consequences of returning blood to the organ

347
00:20:52,100 --> 00:20:55,440
is that once blood returns to the organ,

348
00:20:55,680 --> 00:20:58,140
now oxygen has also returned to the organ,

349
00:20:58,280 --> 00:21:01,120
and inflammatory cells have also returned to the organ,

350
00:21:01,320 --> 00:21:06,640
and the combination of the inflammatory cells, uh, reacting against the dead tissue,

351
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:10,300
along with the presence of oxygen, can actually generate free radicals.

352
00:21:10,660 --> 00:21:15,680
And the generation of free radicals can then further damage the, um,

353
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:18,360
the, the cardiac myocytes in the example of the heart.

354
00:21:18,780 --> 00:21:22,440
And so what'll happen are the classic clinical vignette that, that occurs,

355
00:21:22,840 --> 00:21:25,920
or that can be given, is that there's a patient, they get an MI,

356
00:21:26,420 --> 00:21:31,380
the cardiac enzymes are going up, um, they're taken to the cardiac cath lab,

357
00:21:31,380 --> 00:21:35,420
and, um, the artery is opened, and after the artery is opened,

358
00:21:35,560 --> 00:21:37,560
the cardiac enzymes continue to rise.

359
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,040
Now why do the cardiac enzymes continue to rise?

360
00:21:40,240 --> 00:21:42,940
They continue to rise because once the blood is returned,

361
00:21:43,100 --> 00:21:45,460
the blood contains oxygen and inflammatory cells,

362
00:21:45,660 --> 00:21:49,100
and that's going to result in a generation of free radicals,

363
00:21:49,100 --> 00:21:51,620
which is then going to, uh, going to, um,

364
00:21:51,740 --> 00:21:56,200
allow for continued injury to actually occur to the myocardium.

365
00:21:56,200 --> 00:22:01,520
And that's called a reperfusion injury, i.e., injury that arises due to reperfusion.

366
00:22:01,980 --> 00:22:06,620
So classic vignette is a continued elevation in cardiac enzymes

367
00:22:06,620 --> 00:22:10,560
after opening or returning of the blood supply to the region,

368
00:22:10,760 --> 00:22:14,820
and it is basically due to free radical generation.

369
00:22:16,340 --> 00:22:18,340
So that closes off this section.

